{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe Gerrit Verified gate takes 21-47 minutes end-to-end. Measured on run #1823 (id 30569775555), the critical path is a SINGLE job: `pytest (macos-latest, py3.13)` at 18.6m. Every other job is <=8m; if macOS vanished the run would finish in ~8m.\n\nWorse, macOS runner concurrency is capped (peak 4 concurrent macOS jobs observed across 25 sampled jobs from the last 30 runs). When 6 changes were dispatched within 10 seconds, the later ones queued 6.7-8.4m for a macOS runner. That queue is what turns a 21m run into the 41-47m the operator observes. So macOS job DURATION is doubly valuable: it is both the critical path and the thing holding a scarce runner slot other changes wait on.\n\nWithin that job, the `--cov` default-suite step is 15.4m of the 18.6m. The macOS cells pass `--cov=rebar --cov-report=term-missing:skip-covered`, so coverage.py traces the entire run — but NOTHING consumes the macOS coverage number. The `fail_under = 70` floor in `[tool.coverage.report]` is enforced identically by the three ubuntu cells running the exact same test selection.\n\n## Approach\nMake the coverage flags conditional on the matrix OS in `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml`: ubuntu cells keep `--cov=rebar --cov-report=term-missing:skip-covered`, macOS cells run without them. This is pure duplicate-work removal — the identical 7856 tests still execute on macOS, and the coverage floor is still gated three times over on ubuntu.\n\nRejected alternative: dropping the macOS cell entirely. That is the obvious 2x win and it removes exactly the platform-compat coverage (flock/mkdir lock, gtimeout) the cell exists for. Out of bounds — the requirement is no coverage loss.\n\nRejected alternative: keep `--cov` on macOS but add `--cov-fail-under=0`. Pointless: it retains the tracing cost, which IS the cost, and only removes an assertion that already passes.\n\n## Scope\n- `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml` — the `test` job default-suite step only. The integration-tier step already passes no `--cov` and is untouched. The `-n` worker counts, `--dist` modes, `--basetemp`, and every drift gate are untouched.\n- `docs/coverage.md` — record that coverage is collected on the ubuntu cells only, and why.\n\nExplicitly NOT in scope: sharding the macOS job, changing `-n`, and the verify-identity partial-clone fix (recorded on epic 42eb-5789-c798-4dfc). Those are separate changes with their own risk profiles.\n\n## Testing\nNo new testable behavior — this is a CI workflow configuration change, so no new unit test is warranted. `tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py` pins the substring `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"` (line 67), which this change preserves; that suite must stay green. Verification is observational on the next Verified run: the macOS default-suite step gets faster with an unchanged pass count, and the ubuntu cells still print the coverage report.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The macOS matrix cells run the default suite with no `--cov` or `--cov-report` flags.\n- [ ] The ubuntu matrix cells still pass `--cov=rebar --cov-report=term-missing:skip-covered`.\n- [ ] The `-m \"not integration and not external\"` selection is byte-identical on both platforms, so the set of tests executed on macOS is unchanged.\n- [ ] `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py` passes.\n- [ ] `make lint` passes.\n- [ ] `make typecheck` passes.\n- [ ] `docs/coverage.md` states that CI collects coverage on the ubuntu cells only."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785438802276508001,"uuid":"4063cfab-e1a1-4b8d-9a6c-3b4980a8733d"}